1
00:00:16,224 --> 00:00:19,185
Morning, boss.
Victim's name was Walton Parsell.

2
00:00:19,727 --> 00:00:21,604
Owner of the jewelry store
found him here this morning.

3
00:00:22,063 --> 00:00:24,982
Says Walton bought a woman's necklace
from her around closing time last night.

4
00:00:25,233 --> 00:00:26,275
So not a robbery.

5
00:00:26,651 --> 00:00:28,819
No. Local cops say there was
a jewelry store burglary

6
00:00:28,945 --> 00:00:30,905
across town last month.
No signs of a break-in.

7
00:00:31,113 --> 00:00:32,365
Check the surveillance footage.

8
00:00:32,823 --> 00:00:33,950
What do we know about the victim?

9
00:00:34,158 --> 00:00:36,369
He's a prison guard from Carson,
just outside of town.

10
00:00:36,953 --> 00:00:38,204
- You sure?
- Yeah.

11
00:00:40,039 --> 00:00:42,041
- What?
- Oh, okay. It's in his hands.

12
00:00:43,626 --> 00:00:45,336
Yeah, bit nails, not too calloused.

13
00:00:45,753 --> 00:00:49,215
Blue collar job, high stress
but no heavy lifting. Prison guard.

14
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:50,841
Thought I was losing my touch
there for a second.

15
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:52,260
God forbid.

16
00:00:53,302 --> 00:00:54,345
Clothes tell a different story.

17
00:00:54,470 --> 00:00:56,639
Check out those formal but,
uh, comfortable boots

18
00:00:56,764 --> 00:00:58,140
and the cheap business suit.

19
00:00:58,683 --> 00:01:01,936
Looks, uh, looks a little like a detective.
Don't you think?

20
00:01:02,103 --> 00:01:03,354
- He kinda does, doesn't he?
- Yeah.

21
00:01:03,521 --> 00:01:04,772
Hey, this is not a cheap suit.

22
00:01:04,897 --> 00:01:07,441
Oh, hey, hey, I'm not saying
anything about your suit.

23
00:01:07,567 --> 00:01:08,818
I'm sure it's not. You're the real deal.

24
00:01:09,110 --> 00:01:12,655
But this man here... he
was...he was aspirational.

25
00:01:13,114 --> 00:01:15,533
He had hopes to be a lot more
than a prison guard.

26
00:01:15,908 --> 00:01:18,452
- Too bad the job killed him.
- The job? How so?

27
00:01:18,703 --> 00:01:21,122
Well, he worked at a prison.
That's where they keep killers.

28
00:01:22,123 --> 00:01:24,417
An ex-inmate... probably our best bet.

29
00:01:24,917 --> 00:01:27,670
There are a lot of stab wounds.
That's how they learn to kill in prison.

30
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:29,380
Prison shanks have points, not blades.

31
00:01:29,589 --> 00:01:31,716
They use 'em to puncture
bowels, cause sepsis.

32
00:01:32,633 --> 00:01:35,011
Charming. Well, the question is this...

33
00:01:35,344 --> 00:01:39,307
why would you use a prison
shank outside of prison?

34
00:01:41,434 --> 00:01:44,020
I'll run a check of all the ex-inmates
from Carson in the area.

35
00:01:50,693 --> 00:01:51,736
It's a wedding invitation.

36
00:01:52,069 --> 00:01:53,821
- Is there a dinner?
- Salmon or prime rib.

37
00:01:54,030 --> 00:01:55,072
I'm in.

38
00:01:56,991 --> 00:01:58,743
- Who am I sitting with?
- You're next to Cho.

39
00:02:01,996 --> 00:02:03,581
Where are we with Walton Parsell?

40
00:02:03,831 --> 00:02:05,374
Grew up outside of Hangtree.
Parents are dead.

41
00:02:05,625 --> 00:02:06,709
No close relations nearby.

42
00:02:06,959 --> 00:02:08,753
Started up at Carson on his 21st birthday.

43
00:02:09,462 --> 00:02:11,756
I used his call history
to track down a girlfriend...

44
00:02:11,964 --> 00:02:13,799
Shelley Dillion.
She's a hairdresser in town.

45
00:02:14,133 --> 00:02:16,510
I'll take Jane and go talk to her.
Cho, you go to the prison.

46
00:02:16,927 --> 00:02:19,388
Where are we with Jane's hunch
that there's an ex-con involved?

47
00:02:19,722 --> 00:02:21,474
The prison sent over the
list you asked for.

48
00:02:21,724 --> 00:02:24,310
Ex-Carson inmates with violent records
still living in the county.

49
00:02:24,644 --> 00:02:26,729
If Jane's right
one of these guys is responsible.

50
00:02:26,854 --> 00:02:28,439
It's probably shoe-leather,
but somebody should check it out.

51
00:02:28,606 --> 00:02:31,233
- Take a look at that? I'm on it.
- All right.

52
00:02:31,734 --> 00:02:32,777
Want a partner?

53
00:02:34,987 --> 00:02:36,030
Guess not.

54
00:03:02,181 --> 00:03:04,266
I need to talk to you about a murder
investigation I'm working on.

55
00:03:05,685 --> 00:03:08,938
Keep walking, pig. I got nothin' for you.

56
00:03:12,650 --> 00:03:15,111
Hello, dad. Good to see you, too.

57
00:03:39,842 --> 00:03:42,970
{\an8}- You're still here, huh? You want a beer?
- No.

58
00:03:44,012 --> 00:03:45,055
{\an8}'Course not.

59
00:03:47,558 --> 00:03:49,268
{\an8}Surprised you even know
where to find me.

60
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:52,604
Oh, I make it my business to know exactly
where you are at all times.

61
00:03:52,980 --> 00:03:57,359
Really? Keepin' tabs on me, huh?
Ain't that sweet?

62
00:03:57,943 --> 00:03:59,403
{\an8}So you still in Sacramento?

63
00:04:00,404 --> 00:04:03,365
{\an8}I just need to ask you a few questions.
Procedure.

64
00:04:04,742 --> 00:04:06,952
{\an8}I'm talking to everyone on this
list of ex-Carson inmates.

65
00:04:09,413 --> 00:04:13,250
{\an8}Cops. That guard's been
dead less than a day

66
00:04:14,042 --> 00:04:15,586
{\an8}and you're already grasping at straws.

67
00:04:15,878 --> 00:04:16,920
So you know about the case.

68
00:04:17,504 --> 00:04:21,717
{\an8}Know about it? Hell, boy, walking
around town ten minutes

69
00:04:21,884 --> 00:04:23,343
{\an8}I could tell you who the killer is.

70
00:04:24,178 --> 00:04:25,304
{\an8}But not cops.

71
00:04:25,763 --> 00:04:26,972
They got procedures.

72
00:04:28,682 --> 00:04:32,102
{\an8}- Look, I... I have to know if you were...
- I get it.

73
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:36,690
{\an8}No, sir, officer, I did not
kill anyone last night.

74
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:41,195
{\an8}- Then tell me where you were.
- Cat and the Fiddle on Delaware.

75
00:04:42,029 --> 00:04:43,530
{\an8}They all remember me.

76
00:04:47,284 --> 00:04:49,870
{\an8}- You working?
- I get by.

77
00:04:50,704 --> 00:04:53,081
{\an8}Smuggling cigarettes?
Dealing Meth again?

78
00:04:54,917 --> 00:04:58,629
{\an8}My own flesh and blood... a lousy cop.

79
00:05:00,339 --> 00:05:01,381
Makes me wanna puke.

80
00:05:01,507 --> 00:05:03,217
I just don't wanna see you back in jail.
That's all.

81
00:05:03,467 --> 00:05:06,386
{\an8}You always were a self-righteous
little bastard.

82
00:05:07,179 --> 00:05:09,097
{\an8}That comes from your mom's
side of the family.

83
00:05:10,724 --> 00:05:13,060
No matter how many times
I straightened you out

84
00:05:13,685 --> 00:05:15,103
I never could fix you of that.

85
00:05:16,146 --> 00:05:17,231
It's in the blood, I guess.

86
00:05:18,148 --> 00:05:19,900
{\an8}If you have nothing more to say
about Walton Parsell

87
00:05:21,568 --> 00:05:22,611
{\an8}thanks for your time.

88
00:05:28,784 --> 00:05:32,371
{\an8}I guess I'm in shock.
I feel like I'm numb, you know?

89
00:05:32,746 --> 00:05:35,123
- How long had you known Walton?
- Two months.

90
00:05:35,916 --> 00:05:37,376
That's pretty long for me.

91
00:05:37,668 --> 00:05:38,919
And when was the last time you saw him?

92
00:05:39,253 --> 00:05:42,005
{\an8}Two nights ago. We had dinner out...

93
00:05:42,506 --> 00:05:44,174
quick Chinese, nothing special.

94
00:05:46,301 --> 00:05:47,886
He was supposed to call me last night.

95
00:05:48,637 --> 00:05:49,847
But he didn't.

96
00:05:50,514 --> 00:05:52,432
I thought he was done with me.

97
00:05:53,058 --> 00:05:54,184
You know, like that was it.

98
00:05:55,394 --> 00:05:58,146
Walton had bought a gold necklace
just before he died

99
00:05:59,147 --> 00:06:00,983
and you were worried
that he was gonna leave you?

100
00:06:01,108 --> 00:06:02,526
Because he was a good person?

101
00:06:03,026 --> 00:06:06,029
- He wasn't your usual type, was he?
- Yeah, tell me about it.

102
00:06:06,822 --> 00:06:08,907
I don't know if I'll meet another guy
like Walton.

103
00:06:09,116 --> 00:06:12,411
Yes, your taste for bad boys

104
00:06:12,828 --> 00:06:15,122
is a sad relic of your
childhood mistreatment.

105
00:06:15,330 --> 00:06:16,623
No need to pick it back up again.

106
00:06:16,832 --> 00:06:18,876
Oh, gee, thanks for the advice, Dr. Phil.

107
00:06:19,209 --> 00:06:21,962
Just because you've heard it before
doesn't mean it's not good advice.

108
00:06:22,921 --> 00:06:25,591
Tell us about Walton. Walton had a plan.

109
00:06:26,425 --> 00:06:27,467
He wasn't floating.

110
00:06:27,759 --> 00:06:30,971
He was taking night classes
at the community college...

111
00:06:32,097 --> 00:06:33,891
- criminology.
- Wanted to be a detective.

112
00:06:35,267 --> 00:06:38,729
Can you think of anybody who may
have wanted to hurt Walton?

113
00:06:39,271 --> 00:06:40,314
No.

114
00:06:41,064 --> 00:06:42,774
No, nobody.

115
00:06:43,901 --> 00:06:47,070
Okay. Well, if you think of anything else
just give us a call.

116
00:06:47,195 --> 00:06:48,238
I will.

117
00:06:49,823 --> 00:06:51,825
Oh, Shelley, there is one more thing.

118
00:06:52,242 --> 00:06:54,369
Have you ever stolen supplies
from work here?

119
00:06:56,204 --> 00:06:57,247
No.

120
00:06:57,372 --> 00:06:59,291
No. Of course not.

121
00:06:59,458 --> 00:07:00,500
Good girl.

122
00:07:06,965 --> 00:07:08,216
Walton meant well. That's for sure.

123
00:07:09,426 --> 00:07:10,469
But?

124
00:07:11,803 --> 00:07:13,597
Well, he had these dreams
of being a detective.

125
00:07:15,307 --> 00:07:17,142
He just didn't get
that's not what we do here.

126
00:07:17,559 --> 00:07:19,937
We're not detectives. We're zookeepers.

127
00:07:20,395 --> 00:07:21,438
He was overzealous.

128
00:07:22,689 --> 00:07:25,317
Look, I don't want my men
looking for trouble.

129
00:07:25,901 --> 00:07:27,527
I want them to finish their shifts

130
00:07:27,778 --> 00:07:29,488
with nobody getting hurt,
including themselves.

131
00:07:30,072 --> 00:07:32,574
Did you know Walton was studying
criminology at the local college?

132
00:07:33,158 --> 00:07:35,077
Yeah. That was a big part of the problem.

133
00:07:35,619 --> 00:07:37,704
That school was just spurring him on.

134
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:40,624
Just last month, he told me
he was already building a case

135
00:07:40,832 --> 00:07:44,044
to transfer one of the inmates
to one of those supermax facilities

136
00:07:44,586 --> 00:07:46,588
getting help from one of his professors.

137
00:07:46,964 --> 00:07:48,840
- Which inmate?
- Well, Walton wouldn't tell us.

138
00:07:49,549 --> 00:07:50,759
It's confidential, he says.

139
00:07:52,344 --> 00:07:55,889
He wanted to present his case to us
and, Warden at the next staff meeting.

140
00:07:56,056 --> 00:07:57,683
Did the inmates know
what Walton was doing?

141
00:07:57,975 --> 00:08:00,310
The inmates know everything...
more than me.

142
00:08:01,103 --> 00:08:02,646
That's something else Walton
didn't understand.

143
00:08:10,779 --> 00:08:13,448
And studies constantly show
that eyewitness identification

144
00:08:13,699 --> 00:08:15,742
is wrong as often as it is right

145
00:08:15,993 --> 00:08:18,829
because the human brain
is not a computer.

146
00:08:19,037 --> 00:08:21,540
Can anyone tell me what day of the week

147
00:08:22,124 --> 00:08:25,836
may 31st, 1976 fell on?

148
00:08:26,169 --> 00:08:27,754
- Tell me quickly. You see...
- Monday.

149
00:08:28,547 --> 00:08:29,589
Pardon me?

150
00:08:30,841 --> 00:08:33,635
Monday. It was a Monday.
My brain is a computer.

151
00:08:34,344 --> 00:08:35,512
It's a steel trap.

152
00:08:35,721 --> 00:08:37,889
- A computerized steel trap...
- Professor Huff, hi.

153
00:08:38,015 --> 00:08:40,767
I'm agent Lisbon. This is Patrick Jane.
We're with the CBI.

154
00:08:41,101 --> 00:08:43,228
We're investigating the murder
of Walton Parsell.

155
00:08:43,478 --> 00:08:46,106
Oh, I was very sorry to hear about it.
How can we help you?

156
00:08:46,732 --> 00:08:50,402
We know that he was looking to transfer
an inmate at the prison, and, uh...

157
00:08:50,527 --> 00:08:53,822
Yes. The inmate was involved
in extortion and racketeering

158
00:08:54,072 --> 00:08:57,284
and we analyzed the legal framework
required to take action.

159
00:08:57,492 --> 00:08:58,744
Did he give you the inmate's name?

160
00:08:59,202 --> 00:09:02,122
Well, we deliberately don't use
real names in our conversations.

161
00:09:02,330 --> 00:09:05,042
I'm sure he probably mentioned it
but I don't recall.

162
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,294
I'll take over from here,
if you don't mind.

163
00:09:07,919 --> 00:09:09,212
Hello, class.

164
00:09:09,546 --> 00:09:12,591
Memory is unreliable
because the untrained brain

165
00:09:13,091 --> 00:09:14,384
has a crappy filing system.

166
00:09:14,634 --> 00:09:16,803
It takes everything that happens
to you and throws it all

167
00:09:16,970 --> 00:09:18,096
Willy-nilly into a big, dark closet.

168
00:09:18,263 --> 00:09:20,849
When you go in there looking
for something, all you can find

169
00:09:20,974 --> 00:09:22,893
are the big, obvious things
like when your mind died

170
00:09:23,101 --> 00:09:25,812
or stuff that you don't really need
stuff that you're not looking for

171
00:09:25,937 --> 00:09:28,148
like, uh, like the words to "Copacabana."

172
00:09:28,565 --> 00:09:33,528
You can't find what you need,
but don't panic because it is still there.

173
00:09:34,362 --> 00:09:38,241
Now if we may my lovely assistant
and I will help you rummage

174
00:09:38,366 --> 00:09:41,161
through your magic closets.

175
00:09:41,453 --> 00:09:45,999
Somewhere in there
lurks a name, so start digging

176
00:09:46,291 --> 00:09:48,668
and sorting and pulling things out

177
00:09:49,044 --> 00:09:50,087
and weeding through.

178
00:09:50,212 --> 00:09:53,465
It may be connected to,
the way that Walton laughed

179
00:09:54,132 --> 00:09:56,802
or the smell of his breath.
You just thought of something.

180
00:09:57,594 --> 00:09:59,387
The inmate must have tattoos.

181
00:09:59,971 --> 00:10:03,600
Walton said the guy beat up a tattooist
for spelling "Aryan" wrong.

182
00:10:03,809 --> 00:10:05,477
There we go. Excellent.

183
00:10:06,103 --> 00:10:09,189
Walton asked me questions
about crime in my hometown, Fontana.

184
00:10:09,397 --> 00:10:12,109
Very good.
We're getting somewhere here.

185
00:10:12,317 --> 00:10:14,277
Just let your mind go wherever it wants.

186
00:10:14,653 --> 00:10:17,239
He may have lent you a pen.
He may have worn an ugly shirt.

187
00:10:17,739 --> 00:10:21,660
No, no, he was very buttoned up
and very neat like a soldier.

188
00:10:22,828 --> 00:10:25,413
Like a-a Roman soldier. A general.

189
00:10:25,747 --> 00:10:26,790
Caesar.

190
00:10:26,915 --> 00:10:27,958
Julius...

191
00:10:28,083 --> 00:10:29,960
I know, wait...Brutus...

192
00:10:30,085 --> 00:10:32,129
- Marcus. The inmate's name was Marcus.
- Marcus.

193
00:10:32,254 --> 00:10:34,089
So we're looking for an inmate
with white gang tattoos

194
00:10:34,214 --> 00:10:36,675
who's from Fontana whose name is Marcus.
That'll help.

195
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:40,804
Excellent work. "A"-plus.
Class dismissed. No homework.

196
00:10:42,639 --> 00:10:45,183
Her name was Lola.

197
00:10:45,767 --> 00:10:46,810
She was a showgirl.

198
00:10:50,438 --> 00:10:54,109
Marcus Lansdale... he was a member
of the white trash lowriders from Fontana

199
00:10:54,234 --> 00:10:55,527
when he shot a highway patrolman

200
00:10:55,652 --> 00:10:57,696
during a routine traffic
stop ten years ago.

201
00:10:58,029 --> 00:11:00,282
- Patrolman's in a wheelchair.
- Looks like Walton picked a winner.

202
00:11:00,532 --> 00:11:02,951
They suspect him of running
the prison's protection racket...

203
00:11:03,076 --> 00:11:05,620
protects the drug dealers from the gangs
takes care of snitches

204
00:11:05,829 --> 00:11:06,872
but they can't prove any of it.

205
00:11:06,997 --> 00:11:09,916
If Walton was asking questions
Lansdale probably heard about it.

206
00:11:10,041 --> 00:11:11,459
Jane and I will go talk to him.

207
00:11:12,002 --> 00:11:14,254
If he's involved that means
there's a hit man on the outside.

208
00:11:14,588 --> 00:11:15,630
Hey Rigsby.

209
00:11:15,881 --> 00:11:17,883
Jane's hunch about the
ex-Carson inmate pay off?

210
00:11:18,133 --> 00:11:20,051
I checked out everyone on the list.
Nothing hinky.

211
00:11:20,385 --> 00:11:22,804
All right, we need to check into
Lansdale's known associates.

212
00:11:23,138 --> 00:11:26,183
- Boss, can I, talk to you for a minute?
- Sure.

213
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:28,351
- In your office.
- All right.

214
00:11:36,610 --> 00:11:38,403
I've never talked to you about my father.

215
00:11:39,154 --> 00:11:40,197
Sit down.

216
00:11:43,033 --> 00:11:45,285
I know about your dad. I'm your boss.

217
00:11:46,494 --> 00:11:50,665
Steven Robert Rigsby, convicted
of manslaughter, assault, drug dealing.

218
00:11:52,876 --> 00:11:54,794
Your father was on that list, wasn't he?

219
00:11:56,004 --> 00:11:58,215
I checked out his alibi.
No special treatment.

220
00:11:58,423 --> 00:12:00,926
- If you wanna send someone else...
- I trust you, Rigsby.

221
00:12:01,843 --> 00:12:04,179
However, if you ever come
into contact with your father

222
00:12:04,304 --> 00:12:07,390
on a case, next time, you
bring somebody with you.

223
00:12:07,724 --> 00:12:08,975
Yeah, okay.

224
00:12:09,476 --> 00:12:12,229
You need to get a list of
Lansdale's known associates.

225
00:12:13,063 --> 00:12:15,523
- Get it done by morning.
- You got it, boss.

226
00:12:26,576 --> 00:12:28,411
We're here to talk to you
about Walton Parsell.

227
00:12:28,954 --> 00:12:31,248
- We've read your files...
- I haven't. I'm...I'm getting to it.

228
00:12:31,539 --> 00:12:33,291
I've read your file.

229
00:12:33,750 --> 00:12:36,127
I know that you're running
the protection racket here at Carson

230
00:12:36,419 --> 00:12:38,922
and that Walton was planning on moving you
to a higher security prison.

231
00:12:39,589 --> 00:12:42,300
California law used to say that a man

232
00:12:42,801 --> 00:12:46,304
sentenced to life in prison
is legally dead.

233
00:12:46,972 --> 00:12:49,224
That's a fact. Legally dead.

234
00:12:51,101 --> 00:12:52,143
And...

235
00:12:52,519 --> 00:12:54,938
I've been a dead man for ten years.

236
00:12:55,563 --> 00:12:57,774
The outside world's got no hold on me.

237
00:12:58,149 --> 00:13:01,903
You think some screw poking
into my business bothers me?

238
00:13:02,153 --> 00:13:05,198
Aw, you're not so tough.
There's always bigger fish.

239
00:13:05,448 --> 00:13:06,783
Not in my pond.

240
00:13:06,950 --> 00:13:08,368
Well, Walton wanted you transferred.

241
00:13:08,493 --> 00:13:11,371
You might have ended up in a pond
with a giant pike

242
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:13,873
or snapping turtles.

243
00:13:14,666 --> 00:13:16,918
I have defended myself very successfully

244
00:13:17,627 --> 00:13:20,797
against charges that I am a security risk.

245
00:13:21,673 --> 00:13:25,385
So whatever Parsell wanted to throw at me
would've gone the same way.

246
00:13:25,635 --> 00:13:27,220
Did you know what Walton was gonna do?

247
00:13:30,724 --> 00:13:31,808
Not until you told me.

248
00:13:32,100 --> 00:13:34,060
Maybe one of your friends
on the outside told you.

249
00:13:34,477 --> 00:13:36,062
Oh, I can take care of myself.

250
00:13:36,229 --> 00:13:37,856
That's how you ought to know
I didn't kill him.

251
00:13:38,648 --> 00:13:39,691
How so?

252
00:13:41,609 --> 00:13:43,570
Because if I want a guard dead

253
00:13:44,237 --> 00:13:45,905
you will find him in a broom closet

254
00:13:46,364 --> 00:13:49,117
right here in Carson in my cellblock.

255
00:13:49,576 --> 00:13:51,411
I think he's made some good points.

256
00:13:51,661 --> 00:13:55,165
Marcus, it's been fun.
I got places to go and people to meet.

257
00:13:57,542 --> 00:13:58,835
Personally, I think you got the cheekbones

258
00:13:59,085 --> 00:14:00,670
that you can support
something really short.

259
00:14:00,837 --> 00:14:02,922
I mean, it'd look great on you.
You got a great skull.

260
00:14:03,048 --> 00:14:04,549
- I've always said that.
- I really wish you'd stop.

261
00:14:04,841 --> 00:14:06,384
Oh, we brought a photo
as a reference.

262
00:14:07,385 --> 00:14:08,428
- What?
- There.

263
00:14:12,474 --> 00:14:14,768
Why didn't you tell us you
had a brother in prison?

264
00:14:22,857 --> 00:14:25,526
I don't tell anyone about Marcus.
How'd you know?

265
00:14:26,194 --> 00:14:28,654
Well, you share some of
the same recessive genes

266
00:14:28,946 --> 00:14:33,534
same earlobes, and you both
have the same tell before you lie.

267
00:14:34,744 --> 00:14:37,413
You were dating a guy who's
trying to transfer your brother.

268
00:14:37,830 --> 00:14:39,874
- He ends up dead. It doesn't look good.
- Wait, wait, wait.

269
00:14:40,083 --> 00:14:43,127
You think I killed him?
That's...that's crazy.

270
00:14:43,336 --> 00:14:44,879
- Not really.
- Convince us.

271
00:14:45,213 --> 00:14:48,883
- Where were you last night?
- Annie from the salon's bachelorette party.

272
00:14:49,842 --> 00:14:52,595
Anyway, it doesn't matter.
I never would have hurt him.

273
00:14:53,012 --> 00:14:54,138
But you lied to him.

274
00:14:58,643 --> 00:15:02,438
Marcus knew Walton was trying
to get him transferred

275
00:15:02,688 --> 00:15:06,526
so he asked me to get to know him
and see what his weaknesses are.

276
00:15:06,943 --> 00:15:09,112
One day I followed Walton from Carson.

277
00:15:09,737 --> 00:15:12,156
He went to a coffee shop. I went in after.

278
00:15:12,365 --> 00:15:13,741
We got to talking.

279
00:15:14,325 --> 00:15:16,285
We had our first date a few days later.

280
00:15:16,744 --> 00:15:18,037
You thought Walton was a sucker.

281
00:15:18,329 --> 00:15:20,748
He could help your brother out,
maybe get a few free meals.

282
00:15:20,998 --> 00:15:23,626
What you first thought was
weakness you realized was kindness

283
00:15:23,835 --> 00:15:25,086
and then you got to like the guy.

284
00:15:25,920 --> 00:15:27,338
Did Walton find out about you?

285
00:15:28,381 --> 00:15:32,218
No, he never found out,
and I wasn't gonna tell him.

286
00:15:33,803 --> 00:15:35,555
When Marcus called me last week, I told...

287
00:15:42,770 --> 00:15:45,565
You told him you couldn't do
it anymore, and he got mad.

288
00:15:47,066 --> 00:15:48,109
Did he threaten you?

289
00:15:50,194 --> 00:15:54,031
- You don't know what he can do.
- I think we do. Walton's dead.

290
00:15:54,198 --> 00:15:55,575
Your brother couldn't
have done it from prison.

291
00:15:55,783 --> 00:15:57,368
That means Walton's killer
is on the loose.

292
00:15:57,618 --> 00:15:58,953
- Well...
- Well, what?

293
00:15:59,078 --> 00:16:01,581
- Well, I'm not so sure. I mean, this...
- Make a statement.

294
00:16:01,914 --> 00:16:04,417
Maybe we can get your brother to talk
find out who killed Walton.

295
00:16:07,044 --> 00:16:08,087
No.

296
00:16:09,297 --> 00:16:11,507
I'm sorry. That's... that's my brother.

297
00:16:12,884 --> 00:16:13,926
I can't.

298
00:16:19,265 --> 00:16:21,684
I talked to Shelley's co-workers.
Her alibi checks out.

299
00:16:21,976 --> 00:16:23,978
Figures. She didn't feel
right for it anyway.

300
00:16:24,187 --> 00:16:25,980
Still, she could be an accomplice.

301
00:16:26,314 --> 00:16:27,773
- Where's Jane?
- Uh, first thing in the morning

302
00:16:27,982 --> 00:16:29,400
he had Cho take him back to Carson.

303
00:16:29,567 --> 00:16:32,445
Oh, what for? Lansdale isn't talking
and the killer's on the outside.

304
00:16:33,613 --> 00:16:34,655
Whatever.

305
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:36,491
Any breaks on finding Lansdale's hit man?

306
00:16:36,866 --> 00:16:38,201
I talked to Hangtree P.D.

307
00:16:38,326 --> 00:16:40,870
They don't have any ideas about
a murder-for-hire candidate.

308
00:16:41,078 --> 00:16:43,372
Lansdale's known associates on the outside
are a pretty small list.

309
00:16:43,498 --> 00:16:44,540
His whole gang's still in jail.

310
00:16:44,665 --> 00:16:46,834
I read the file from the jewelry store
burglary last month

311
00:16:47,001 --> 00:16:48,336
and there's nothing there.

312
00:16:48,544 --> 00:16:49,754
We need a new angle.

313
00:16:52,089 --> 00:16:53,132
What is it?

314
00:16:53,883 --> 00:16:55,676
There is... one angle I could try.

315
00:17:06,479 --> 00:17:07,688
Well, I thought you'd be back.

316
00:17:09,732 --> 00:17:11,067
But I didn't think you'd bring a gift.

317
00:17:11,567 --> 00:17:13,027
We just need a moment of your time.

318
00:17:16,030 --> 00:17:18,115
- Can I get you somethin' to drink?
- No.

319
00:17:18,658 --> 00:17:21,494
- I wasn't talking to you.
- No, thank you.

320
00:17:22,745 --> 00:17:23,788
You get on that yet?

321
00:17:24,914 --> 00:17:26,332
I warned her you'd be crude.

322
00:17:27,208 --> 00:17:28,918
Couldn't close the deal, huh?

323
00:17:29,168 --> 00:17:31,837
Look, we need your help finding
the killer of Walton Parsell.

324
00:17:32,213 --> 00:17:33,965
Yeah, you said you could find
the killer in ten minutes.

325
00:17:34,423 --> 00:17:36,842
If I wanted to do that, but why
would I wanna do that?

326
00:17:37,093 --> 00:17:38,135
To show me you can.

327
00:17:38,344 --> 00:17:40,012
Show me you're not just full of big talk.

328
00:17:41,973 --> 00:17:45,351
Well, the least you could do is ask nice.
I know you know how to ask nice.

329
00:17:46,727 --> 00:17:49,605
Please, dad. I need your help.

330
00:17:51,357 --> 00:17:55,278
All right. We need to find a woman
by the name of Rocket.

331
00:17:56,153 --> 00:17:57,363
Old flame of mine.

332
00:17:57,530 --> 00:17:59,532
She knows everything
that goes on in Hangtree.

333
00:17:59,865 --> 00:18:02,451
- Rocket? Real name?
- Elaine Hamilton.

334
00:18:02,868 --> 00:18:03,911
Got an address?

335
00:18:04,078 --> 00:18:07,206
Well, I don't know where she sleeps
but I know where she lives...

336
00:18:07,707 --> 00:18:09,542
this jailbird bar in town.

337
00:18:10,126 --> 00:18:11,168
Can you take us to her?

338
00:18:11,419 --> 00:18:13,546
Yeah, I can do that.

339
00:18:14,338 --> 00:18:16,048
Just give me a second. I'll get my keys.

340
00:18:18,676 --> 00:18:20,678
"He was trying to, uh, transfer Lansdale?"

341
00:18:22,847 --> 00:18:24,515
"See, that's Walton for you."

342
00:18:24,807 --> 00:18:26,058
"Meant well, but naive."

343
00:18:26,726 --> 00:18:29,186
Lansdale sounds like a
good target for transfer.

344
00:18:29,895 --> 00:18:31,856
Lansdale is the muscle at Carson.

345
00:18:32,523 --> 00:18:33,649
Nobody's gonna talk to Walton.

346
00:18:33,774 --> 00:18:36,569
They don't wanna find themselves
on the bad end of Lansdale's knife.

347
00:18:37,069 --> 00:18:38,404
He did his own dirty work?

348
00:18:38,779 --> 00:18:41,240
He who holds the knife
gets the respect from the guys.

349
00:18:42,325 --> 00:18:44,035
You wanna stay on top,
you gotta back it up.

350
00:18:45,536 --> 00:18:48,539
This is great. Reality tv at its finest.

351
00:18:49,665 --> 00:18:52,835
Everything that happens in cellblock "c"
is right here on these monitors.

352
00:18:53,294 --> 00:18:56,005
Lansdale's too hard-core for a small place.
You didn't think about transfer yourself?

353
00:18:57,632 --> 00:18:59,216
We're guards, not supermen.

354
00:18:59,634 --> 00:19:03,471
Having a shark like Lansdale in the tank
keeps all these guys in line.

355
00:19:04,263 --> 00:19:06,098
He does your job for you. That's nice.

356
00:19:06,432 --> 00:19:08,809
So you disapproved of Walton
going after Lansdale.

357
00:19:09,060 --> 00:19:10,728
I didn't have any knowledge of that...

358
00:19:11,270 --> 00:19:12,396
Walton's agenda.

359
00:19:12,855 --> 00:19:14,440
That would be supposition.

360
00:19:14,982 --> 00:19:16,192
Okay.

361
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:18,903
Oh, you see these two?

362
00:19:19,945 --> 00:19:21,238
They're about to punch each other.

363
00:19:21,947 --> 00:19:24,075
Look at the way their shoulders are set.
It's very primal.

364
00:19:24,533 --> 00:19:27,244
So are we done here? I got work to do.

365
00:19:27,620 --> 00:19:28,663
Jane, you have anything else?

366
00:19:28,788 --> 00:19:30,373
Oh! There they go.
Look, look, look, look. Fight.

367
00:19:31,332 --> 00:19:33,501
We got a fight in northwest hallway "B."

368
00:19:34,794 --> 00:19:37,171
Somebody break it up.
Okay, I'm on my way.

369
00:19:37,922 --> 00:19:39,507
Lock the... lock the hallway down.

370
00:19:41,550 --> 00:19:43,094
Jane, unless you have something,
we need to get out of here.

371
00:19:43,344 --> 00:19:45,346
No, you go.
I'll...I'll get a ride home somehow.

372
00:19:45,638 --> 00:19:48,140
- This is way too good.
- All right.

373
00:19:48,891 --> 00:19:49,934
Way too good.

374
00:20:36,147 --> 00:20:38,649
- You gonna be here all day?
- Oh, there you are.

375
00:20:40,109 --> 00:20:42,069
I don't wanna scare you, but, uh...

376
00:20:46,031 --> 00:20:47,867
I did some time myself once.

377
00:20:50,161 --> 00:20:51,203
Really?

378
00:20:51,495 --> 00:20:52,538
Oh, yeah.

379
00:20:52,663 --> 00:20:57,376
Anyway, when I was inside
there were prisoners with privileges.

380
00:20:57,501 --> 00:20:59,128
- What do you call those... trustees?
- Yeah.

381
00:20:59,378 --> 00:21:00,838
- You have some here?
- Yeah, we have a few.

382
00:21:00,963 --> 00:21:02,006
I'd like to talk to them.

383
00:21:03,924 --> 00:21:05,926
Okay. Let's go.

384
00:21:07,094 --> 00:21:08,387
Those guys in the blue...

385
00:21:09,180 --> 00:21:12,016
that's trustees from cellblock "c."
That's Walton's block.

386
00:21:15,019 --> 00:21:18,481
The bald man with the tattoo...
he got long left on his sentence?

387
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:21,776
Velasquez. Down to his last year.

388
00:21:23,194 --> 00:21:24,278
I'll take him.

389
00:21:25,404 --> 00:21:27,656
Jorge! Over here.

390
00:21:28,657 --> 00:21:30,451
Let's take two. Keep 'em honest.

391
00:21:31,243 --> 00:21:33,746
Uh... the guy on the other end.
Other side of the table.

392
00:21:34,205 --> 00:21:36,040
Nick Monaco. Another short-timer.

393
00:21:36,791 --> 00:21:37,917
He's not a murderer, is he?

394
00:21:38,375 --> 00:21:40,252
No, Nicky's a second-story man.

395
00:21:40,836 --> 00:21:42,922
A thief. Got clean hands.

396
00:21:44,131 --> 00:21:45,174
Him, too.

397
00:21:45,341 --> 00:21:46,884
Nicky! Over here.

398
00:21:56,644 --> 00:21:57,937
This is the place all right.

399
00:21:58,938 --> 00:22:00,648
- You wait here. I'll go get her.
- No, we'll get her.

400
00:22:02,274 --> 00:22:05,319
You two go walking in there looking like
the cop family Robinson

401
00:22:05,444 --> 00:22:07,780
the joint will clear out
and you'll never find Rocket.

402
00:22:09,990 --> 00:22:11,534
Okay. You're not back in two minutes,
we're coming in.

403
00:22:12,034 --> 00:22:13,118
You got it, officer.

404
00:22:13,911 --> 00:22:14,954
Yes, sir.

405
00:22:15,913 --> 00:22:17,957
Yes, sir. 'Sup, Peckerwoods?

406
00:22:21,502 --> 00:22:23,838
You know that game that kids play...

407
00:22:24,046 --> 00:22:25,881
"My dad can beat up your dad"?

408
00:22:27,842 --> 00:22:29,051
No one ever played that with me.

409
00:22:30,803 --> 00:22:31,846
He's a...

410
00:22:33,055 --> 00:22:35,224
- Character.
- Yep.

411
00:22:35,724 --> 00:22:38,269
Maybe this will bring you
two closer together.

412
00:22:38,477 --> 00:22:39,520
Don't hold your breath.

413
00:22:39,645 --> 00:22:40,688
Hey, put it down!

414
00:22:41,146 --> 00:22:42,189
You get her. I'm going in.

415
00:22:43,732 --> 00:22:44,775
Stop!

416
00:22:49,405 --> 00:22:51,740
So bad now... lower your weapon now!

417
00:22:51,866 --> 00:22:54,577
- Get on outta here, boy.
- I'm a cop. Everybody stay calm.

418
00:22:55,077 --> 00:22:58,706
He's got a knife. Yeah, I can see that.
What the hell's going on in here?

419
00:22:58,998 --> 00:23:02,835
Seems Andy over here was a little mad
over the last time we crossed paths.

420
00:23:03,669 --> 00:23:06,881
Wanted to fistfight,
only I don't do fistfights.

421
00:23:07,298 --> 00:23:10,968
- A waste of time. Right, Andy?
- Let me go. Please?

422
00:23:11,343 --> 00:23:13,596
Just put the knife down!
Let me handle this.

423
00:23:13,929 --> 00:23:15,055
The hell with that.

424
00:23:15,222 --> 00:23:18,642
Dad, nobody needs to get cut
and nobody needs to get shot.

425
00:23:19,226 --> 00:23:21,228
Just put the knife down.

426
00:23:28,736 --> 00:23:31,655
Guess Old Andy here ain't gonna be
taking any more pokes at me.

427
00:23:31,822 --> 00:23:32,865
That's for sure.

428
00:23:33,198 --> 00:23:34,992
You, get outta here.

429
00:23:37,119 --> 00:23:38,162
Your turn.

430
00:23:39,246 --> 00:23:40,956
Put the gun away,
and we'll call this a wash.

431
00:23:43,542 --> 00:23:44,585
Better.

432
00:23:49,924 --> 00:23:50,966
Let's go.

433
00:23:56,305 --> 00:23:57,848
What are you trying to do, embarrass me?

434
00:23:58,349 --> 00:24:00,517
Never get between me
and my business again.

435
00:24:00,643 --> 00:24:01,685
I just saved your life in there.

436
00:24:01,852 --> 00:24:04,104
What, that clown?
He wasn't gonna pull the trigger.

437
00:24:04,271 --> 00:24:05,648
Really? And how'd you know?

438
00:24:06,190 --> 00:24:09,568
Weakness, son.
Weakness. Lives in the eyes.

439
00:24:11,904 --> 00:24:14,573
Meet Rocket.
She's faster than she looks.

440
00:24:15,908 --> 00:24:18,494
- What the hell, Rigs?
- I told you to play nice.

441
00:24:19,161 --> 00:24:21,121
You know something about a prison guard
getting killed in town?

442
00:24:21,372 --> 00:24:23,457
No. No, sir, I do not.

443
00:24:24,416 --> 00:24:25,834
If I'm lyin', I'm dyin'.

444
00:24:26,543 --> 00:24:27,670
Tell 'em.

445
00:24:28,295 --> 00:24:29,338
Tell 'em.

446
00:24:31,924 --> 00:24:34,843
Butch Carwin.
Came into the bar last night.

447
00:24:35,386 --> 00:24:37,346
Flashed a wad of cash
that would choke a mule.

448
00:24:38,514 --> 00:24:40,474
Said he dropped some prison
guard for the dough.

449
00:24:40,724 --> 00:24:42,101
Where can we find Carwin?

450
00:24:42,601 --> 00:24:46,814
Well, he owns some land out
near the quarry off route 23.

451
00:24:47,314 --> 00:24:49,483
All right. Thanks for your assistance.

452
00:24:56,198 --> 00:24:57,616
You some kind of V.I.P. or somethin'?

453
00:24:57,950 --> 00:25:01,912
Something. You boys can relax.
I'm an ex-con myself.

454
00:25:02,079 --> 00:25:05,082
Writing wolf tickets,
talking out your neck. I'm hip.

455
00:25:05,582 --> 00:25:08,043
- Whatever you say, pal.
- Okay.

456
00:25:08,460 --> 00:25:11,046
That's an interesting tattoo.
Looks fresh. You just get that?

457
00:25:11,505 --> 00:25:14,466
Yeah. My cousin just landed
in here for drugs.

458
00:25:14,800 --> 00:25:16,760
- Nice.
- He's a real artist.

459
00:25:17,302 --> 00:25:18,929
Well, maybe Nick could use his services.

460
00:25:19,054 --> 00:25:21,098
Not me. I'm not into all that ink.

461
00:25:21,598 --> 00:25:23,392
Old school. I get it.

462
00:25:24,393 --> 00:25:25,894
I take care of myself. So what?

463
00:25:26,103 --> 00:25:27,146
Nothing wrong with that.

464
00:25:28,856 --> 00:25:32,109
- What's through there?
- It's a loading bay for the laundry.

465
00:25:32,776 --> 00:25:34,236
Let's go take a look, shall we?

466
00:25:41,618 --> 00:25:44,872
Do the laundry for every hotel
and restaurant in Hangtree.

467
00:25:45,497 --> 00:25:48,375
Wow. Must keep the prisoners very busy.

468
00:25:50,419 --> 00:25:51,670
Three shifts a day.

469
00:25:51,962 --> 00:25:54,381
Yeah, all us trustees work as supervisors
here in the laundry.

470
00:25:55,132 --> 00:25:57,134
Must know the movers and shakers
around here, huh?

471
00:25:57,676 --> 00:25:58,802
Guys like Lansdale.

472
00:26:04,850 --> 00:26:06,351
I know to mind my own business.

473
00:26:10,856 --> 00:26:13,442
You know, you seem like a smart guy.

474
00:26:13,817 --> 00:26:15,486
- Don't be an idiot.
- Excuse me?

475
00:26:15,986 --> 00:26:17,654
If you know something

476
00:26:17,863 --> 00:26:20,282
maybe about Lansdale
and his reign of terror

477
00:26:20,407 --> 00:26:23,827
then tell me.
I can get your sentence shortened.

478
00:26:25,829 --> 00:26:26,872
Listen here.

479
00:26:27,623 --> 00:26:30,959
I got six months left in this joint
and then I'm free.

480
00:26:32,127 --> 00:26:35,089
I'd like to spend those
six months unstabbed.

481
00:26:35,339 --> 00:26:37,341
I get it. I've been down.

482
00:26:38,425 --> 00:26:39,927
Snitches get stitches.

483
00:26:40,594 --> 00:26:42,638
Oh, see, that's a popular misconception.

484
00:26:43,764 --> 00:26:46,016
The stitches are for live people.

485
00:26:47,101 --> 00:26:48,143
Got it.

486
00:26:55,651 --> 00:26:56,735
Is that him?

487
00:26:57,402 --> 00:26:59,154
Mask doesn't help
but yeah, I think so.

488
00:26:59,613 --> 00:27:02,324
Looks like butch Carwin's got himself
a good old-fashioned meth lab.

489
00:27:03,992 --> 00:27:05,285
And he's armed.

490
00:27:06,453 --> 00:27:08,122
That's probable cause.
We can take him in right now.

491
00:27:08,580 --> 00:27:10,958
- We should call for backup.
- No need. This is gonna be easy.

492
00:27:11,083 --> 00:27:12,501
You don't need to prove
anything to anyone.

493
00:27:13,919 --> 00:27:16,338
Your father is the last person in the world
you should be trying to impress.

494
00:27:16,880 --> 00:27:19,508
You're a good person in spite of him.
You don't owe him anything.

495
00:27:19,925 --> 00:27:20,968
You got me wrong.

496
00:27:21,093 --> 00:27:22,136
I don't give a damn about my dad.

497
00:27:22,261 --> 00:27:24,680
I'm just saying we can take
Carwin down now, no problem.

498
00:27:58,547 --> 00:28:01,133
CBI! Down to the ground, Carwin!
Get down!

499
00:28:01,633 --> 00:28:02,759
- On the ground!
- Who's there?

500
00:28:02,885 --> 00:28:05,387
- Who's there? I can't see anything, man!
- I'll pass. Get down on the ground now!

501
00:28:05,512 --> 00:28:07,055
Okay, okay. Son of a bitch.

502
00:28:07,181 --> 00:28:09,433
Facedown on the ground!
Arms out to the side now.

503
00:28:16,940 --> 00:28:17,983
Pretty good trick.

504
00:28:18,775 --> 00:28:21,486
Yep. Always worked on my dad.

505
00:28:26,740 --> 00:28:29,993
We've got you for production
of methamphetamine and illegal weapons.

506
00:28:30,493 --> 00:28:32,162
Who knows what else we're gonna find?

507
00:28:32,287 --> 00:28:34,581
I'm just no damn good. That's the truth.

508
00:28:36,958 --> 00:28:38,418
You recognize this man?

509
00:28:41,796 --> 00:28:44,716
I might have done a piece
of time with a fella like that once.

510
00:28:46,218 --> 00:28:47,344
Lansdale.

511
00:28:48,386 --> 00:28:49,679
What about this man?

512
00:28:49,930 --> 00:28:52,891
Oh, him I don't know. Looks like a cop.

513
00:28:53,308 --> 00:28:55,602
He's the man you killed
for Marcus Lansdale.

514
00:28:56,645 --> 00:28:58,396
Okay, hold up. I did what now?

515
00:28:58,563 --> 00:29:00,607
Murder for hire. It's a capital crime.

516
00:29:00,982 --> 00:29:03,735
You finger Lansdale, you'll just
go to jail for a long time.

517
00:29:04,027 --> 00:29:05,403
Okay, look, I don't know
who's telling you what

518
00:29:05,528 --> 00:29:07,489
but I've never seen that fella in my life.

519
00:29:07,697 --> 00:29:09,241
Why don't you tell me where
you were two nights ago?

520
00:29:10,450 --> 00:29:11,952
Oh, I was cooking up meth.

521
00:29:14,704 --> 00:29:16,831
Hang tight, Butch.
It's gonna be a long day.

522
00:29:20,919 --> 00:29:22,545
- Hello?
- Lisbon, it's Jane.

523
00:29:22,796 --> 00:29:24,339
I'm in jail. Come join me.

524
00:29:24,714 --> 00:29:25,966
You're still in Carson? Why?

525
00:29:26,258 --> 00:29:28,843
Uh... I'm catching the killer, of course.

526
00:29:29,344 --> 00:29:31,596
Look, we've got a pretty good
suspect in holding here.

527
00:29:31,721 --> 00:29:33,348
You would know that if
you answered your phone.

528
00:29:33,598 --> 00:29:35,684
These places aren't exactly
built for their reception.

529
00:29:36,017 --> 00:29:38,520
Anyway, trust me, Lisbon.

530
00:29:38,937 --> 00:29:40,897
"Get your car keys. Get in your car."

531
00:29:42,357 --> 00:29:43,400
And...

532
00:29:44,442 --> 00:29:45,735
Lisbon? Yeah.

533
00:29:47,821 --> 00:29:48,863
"What's this about?"

534
00:29:48,989 --> 00:29:51,950
Uh, the gentleman with
the shaking leg right there.

535
00:29:53,660 --> 00:29:55,078
Tomas. Come on.

536
00:30:05,046 --> 00:30:06,881
Aw, man.

537
00:30:08,174 --> 00:30:09,217
Looks like heroin.

538
00:30:09,426 --> 00:30:12,637
Uh, and I think if you
check the water fountain

539
00:30:12,762 --> 00:30:14,347
you might find, uh...

540
00:30:15,557 --> 00:30:16,599
Huh?

541
00:30:18,101 --> 00:30:19,144
Bingo.

542
00:30:19,352 --> 00:30:23,398
- It's not mine, man.
- I'm sure it's not. Let's go. Let's go.

543
00:30:24,691 --> 00:30:27,277
I'm so sure. Let's go. Let's go.

544
00:30:31,072 --> 00:30:34,367
You had me drive all the way out here
to watch you bust a drug dealer?

545
00:30:34,492 --> 00:30:35,535
It wasn't a bust.

546
00:30:35,869 --> 00:30:38,371
I just had to check to see
if my informant's info was good.

547
00:30:38,705 --> 00:30:39,748
Informant?

548
00:30:39,873 --> 00:30:42,417
Yeah, I got one of the prisoners talking.
Gonna give us Lansdale.

549
00:30:42,625 --> 00:30:45,420
- Which prisoner did you get talking?
- Nick, the thief.

550
00:30:46,338 --> 00:30:50,216
Yeah. I did the old razzle-dazzle on him.
It was very cop.

551
00:30:50,967 --> 00:30:52,135
He's ready to make a statement

552
00:30:52,344 --> 00:30:54,596
as soon as we can draw up
the paperwork to get him outta here.

553
00:30:54,971 --> 00:30:56,014
Nobody likes a snitch.

554
00:30:56,848 --> 00:30:57,891
Let's get him outta here.

555
00:31:10,278 --> 00:31:11,321
Take it.

556
00:31:11,446 --> 00:31:14,783
The drug bust in the cafeteria...
I know who talked.

557
00:31:15,075 --> 00:31:17,702
- Oh, yeah? Who?
- Nick, the thief.

558
00:31:18,661 --> 00:31:19,704
Nick?

559
00:31:21,373 --> 00:31:23,166
Cop said he's giving you up next.

560
00:31:23,541 --> 00:31:26,086
No, he's not. You get me a knife.

561
00:31:26,336 --> 00:31:29,339
Just before lights out. You be ready.

562
00:31:30,465 --> 00:31:31,508
Lab techs just finished

563
00:31:31,633 --> 00:31:32,967
going through the stuff we
seized from Carwin.

564
00:31:33,218 --> 00:31:34,260
Oh, yeah?

565
00:31:34,386 --> 00:31:36,471
Yeah. It's turning out
to be a pretty nice case.

566
00:31:36,930 --> 00:31:39,182
Meth lab but a garage
full of stolen stereos

567
00:31:39,307 --> 00:31:41,559
and about 600 cartons
of untaxed cigarettes.

568
00:31:41,976 --> 00:31:44,562
- Looks like your dad came through for once.
- Wait. What?

569
00:31:45,105 --> 00:31:46,981
- I said it looks like your dad...
- Untaxed cigarettes?

570
00:31:47,816 --> 00:31:49,609
- Yeah? So?
- Cover for me. I gotta go.

571
00:31:52,946 --> 00:31:53,988
You know?

572
00:31:57,242 --> 00:32:00,286
- Kind of busy here, boy.
- Your lead was crap.

573
00:32:00,412 --> 00:32:03,873
Butch Carwin didn't kill that guard
but he is smuggling cigarettes, like you.

574
00:32:04,582 --> 00:32:07,752
- That's a weird coincidence, huh?
- I think I'll freshen this up.

575
00:32:07,877 --> 00:32:08,920
Yeah, I think you should.

576
00:32:10,922 --> 00:32:12,715
She lied for you to help take out a rival.

577
00:32:13,216 --> 00:32:16,177
Only thing a cop's ever good for...
getting rid of the competition.

578
00:32:17,429 --> 00:32:20,473
Besides, you got yourself a nice, big bust
for you and the redhead.

579
00:32:21,224 --> 00:32:22,434
Everybody's happy.

580
00:32:23,560 --> 00:32:24,727
You used me.

581
00:32:26,020 --> 00:32:27,105
Hey, what's family for?

582
00:32:28,314 --> 00:32:31,526
- You know? I think your mom...
- Don't talk about my mom.

583
00:32:31,651 --> 00:32:33,528
Shut up. You shut up!

584
00:32:34,946 --> 00:32:35,989
Well, now...

585
00:32:37,073 --> 00:32:40,618
Thinking that maybe taking a punch
at the old man will fix you up right?

586
00:32:41,661 --> 00:32:43,163
Give you some inner peace?

587
00:32:44,080 --> 00:32:46,624
But before you swing,
you remember what I said before.

588
00:32:51,254 --> 00:32:52,797
I don't do fistfights.

589
00:32:56,009 --> 00:32:57,177
Come ahead, son.

590
00:33:06,269 --> 00:33:08,104
"Locking up in 15 minutes, gentlemen."

591
00:33:14,444 --> 00:33:15,570
Kill that snitch.

592
00:33:17,071 --> 00:33:18,990
Nick, I'm coming for you.

593
00:33:20,867 --> 00:33:22,410
Drop the knife, Lansdale.

594
00:33:24,913 --> 00:33:26,956
Don't make me have to
pepper-spray your ass.

595
00:33:29,042 --> 00:33:30,084
You know the drill.

596
00:33:39,135 --> 00:33:40,595
This enough to send him to supermax?

597
00:33:41,888 --> 00:33:44,182
I gotta say Walton would have liked
to have seen this.

598
00:33:44,849 --> 00:33:47,894
- Don't worry. We'll pack your bags for you.
- Yeah? Well, you can all go to hell.

599
00:33:48,561 --> 00:33:50,021
And that goes double for Nick.

600
00:33:51,022 --> 00:33:53,525
It's all over this place that
he was the one snitching on me.

601
00:33:54,400 --> 00:33:55,443
Get him outta here.

602
00:33:55,818 --> 00:33:56,861
Get him outta here.

603
00:33:57,654 --> 00:33:59,531
I know somebody's gonna get that rat!

604
00:33:59,656 --> 00:34:01,741
Take him outta here.
Lock the cellblock down.

605
00:34:01,908 --> 00:34:03,409
You better move Nick to an isolation unit.

606
00:34:04,035 --> 00:34:06,329
We put him in the laundry room
on the night shift. Let's go.

607
00:34:09,457 --> 00:34:10,500
Time's up, Nicky.

608
00:34:11,668 --> 00:34:12,710
Nick?

609
00:34:14,837 --> 00:34:15,880
Where'd Nick go?

610
00:34:19,050 --> 00:34:20,093
You seen Nick?

611
00:34:20,218 --> 00:34:21,844
- He was just at the desk.
- Well, he's not now.

612
00:34:22,679 --> 00:34:24,889
We've got a missing convict.
Put us on lockdown.

613
00:34:30,768 --> 00:34:33,521
"Lockdown in cell block C.
Inmate missing."

614
00:34:43,531 --> 00:34:45,866
- Is it a rabbit?
- Yeah, it's a rabbit.

615
00:34:47,910 --> 00:34:48,953
Rabbit dancing?

616
00:34:50,079 --> 00:34:51,414
Rabbits don't dance.

617
00:34:51,539 --> 00:34:53,583
Cho, got our laundry truck.

618
00:34:54,417 --> 00:34:55,459
Guys, light it up.

619
00:35:08,097 --> 00:35:09,974
Sir, I'm gonna need you to step out
of the vehicle.

620
00:35:16,647 --> 00:35:18,316
Olly, olly, oxen free!

621
00:35:20,526 --> 00:35:21,569
Son of bitch!

622
00:35:25,531 --> 00:35:28,618
I had to run.
You made everyone think I was a snitch.

623
00:35:29,076 --> 00:35:31,037
Yeah, well, I'm sorry about that.

624
00:35:31,287 --> 00:35:34,123
My bad, but I just thought
it was the best way

625
00:35:34,248 --> 00:35:35,875
for you to show us how you
sneak in and out of prison.

626
00:35:36,125 --> 00:35:37,793
Just like you did the night
you killed Walton.

627
00:35:39,086 --> 00:35:40,546
I got nothin' to say.

628
00:35:41,130 --> 00:35:43,007
Well, we have ways of making you talk.

629
00:35:54,644 --> 00:35:57,313
- What the hell are you doing here?
- Keeping warm.

630
00:36:03,319 --> 00:36:04,737
You burn my whole stash?

631
00:36:06,822 --> 00:36:07,865
Yup.

632
00:36:10,117 --> 00:36:11,160
Hard-core.

633
00:36:13,287 --> 00:36:15,790
I do you. You do me,
and that's the way it should be.

634
00:36:17,875 --> 00:36:19,335
So does that make us even?

635
00:36:38,020 --> 00:36:39,063
No.

636
00:36:40,606 --> 00:36:41,649
All right, son.

637
00:36:46,821 --> 00:36:48,072
Just this once.

638
00:36:48,614 --> 00:36:49,949
I'll give you a fair chance.

639
00:36:53,160 --> 00:36:54,662
Too much damn cop in you, boy.

640
00:37:00,042 --> 00:37:01,085
We're not done yet.

641
00:37:54,013 --> 00:37:55,097
Should've used the knife.

642
00:38:00,853 --> 00:38:01,896
See you around, son.

643
00:38:09,153 --> 00:38:10,946
The laundry truck driver
is in the other room

644
00:38:11,197 --> 00:38:12,239
singing like a bird.

645
00:38:13,199 --> 00:38:15,868
Save us some time and talk
and we'll move you to the east coast

646
00:38:16,076 --> 00:38:18,287
where nobody knows you're a snitch.

647
00:38:19,330 --> 00:38:20,915
I never snitched.
This guy here made that up.

648
00:38:21,040 --> 00:38:23,959
Oh, well, have fun explaining that
to the boys on the cellblock.

649
00:38:30,466 --> 00:38:33,219
What the hell. I'm cooked, right?

650
00:38:37,515 --> 00:38:39,183
I was supposed to get out in six months.

651
00:38:40,684 --> 00:38:42,436
I wanted to have something waiting for me.

652
00:38:44,605 --> 00:38:46,232
So I found that jewelry store in town.

653
00:38:46,357 --> 00:38:48,943
I promised that driver a share
if he'd sneak me out.

654
00:38:49,860 --> 00:38:52,571
"Yes, his poor taste in jewelry
was a very early clue."

655
00:38:56,826 --> 00:38:58,327
So I robbed the place.

656
00:38:59,787 --> 00:39:01,080
It went smooth.

657
00:39:01,497 --> 00:39:03,833
So I figure, let's do another one.

658
00:39:04,124 --> 00:39:05,584
And that's when you ran into Walton.

659
00:39:06,126 --> 00:39:07,628
Kid didn't have to be a hero.

660
00:39:09,463 --> 00:39:11,173
"He could've just left it alone."

661
00:39:13,300 --> 00:39:15,803
"That's just how he was, you know?"

662
00:39:17,429 --> 00:39:19,265
"He had to try and do the right thing."

663
00:39:29,859 --> 00:39:31,110
Thought I got away clean.

664
00:39:31,819 --> 00:39:33,904
Well, I figured someone that was
getting in and out of prison

665
00:39:34,071 --> 00:39:36,991
must have the trust of the
guards, hence a trustee

666
00:39:37,241 --> 00:39:40,077
and the laundry...
well, that's just the smartest way out.

667
00:39:41,996 --> 00:39:43,330
That could have been any one of us.

668
00:39:43,664 --> 00:39:47,293
Yes, but you and Jorge stood out.
That's why I chose you both.

669
00:39:47,751 --> 00:39:49,670
His tattoo was far better work

670
00:39:49,795 --> 00:39:52,506
than the average prison
work you see, and fresh.

671
00:39:52,631 --> 00:39:55,009
Then he mentioned his cousin,
the artist, which leaves you

672
00:39:55,134 --> 00:39:56,844
and your perfectly manicured digits.

673
00:39:57,052 --> 00:39:59,305
There's not a nail salon
in prison, is there?

674
00:40:06,854 --> 00:40:08,105
I like to look right.

675
00:40:09,189 --> 00:40:10,232
I'm old school.

676
00:40:11,692 --> 00:40:15,195
I never killed anyone on any of my jobs.
I never had to.

677
00:40:16,488 --> 00:40:18,157
I was too good to need violence.

678
00:40:18,699 --> 00:40:20,910
But still, you took a knife with you.

679
00:40:23,370 --> 00:40:25,039
Someone stands in front of you

680
00:40:26,040 --> 00:40:27,499
gonna take away everything

681
00:40:28,375 --> 00:40:29,585
you gotta put him down.

682
00:40:30,336 --> 00:40:33,464
I mean, right or wrong, you put him down.

683
00:40:34,548 --> 00:40:35,591
You get that.

684
00:40:37,051 --> 00:40:38,093
Don't you?

685
00:40:44,224 --> 00:40:45,351
Hey.

686
00:40:46,185 --> 00:40:48,562
Hey. What happened to you tonight?

687
00:40:49,146 --> 00:40:50,439
- Something with your dad?
- Yeah.

688
00:40:51,649 --> 00:40:52,775
I was gonna make coffee.

689
00:40:53,275 --> 00:40:55,402
Do you wanna go to the shop
down the street, talk about it?

690
00:40:55,527 --> 00:40:56,570
No, I'm fine.

691
00:40:57,863 --> 00:40:58,948
I'm better than fine, actually.

692
00:41:00,491 --> 00:41:01,533
All right.

693
00:41:03,410 --> 00:41:04,578
Wait. There's just one more thing.

694
00:41:07,081 --> 00:41:08,666
I'm sorry, but I'm not gonna be
at your wedding.

695
00:41:09,875 --> 00:41:13,087
That's too bad.

696
00:41:14,505 --> 00:41:15,923
You have something else
planned that day?

697
00:41:17,174 --> 00:41:20,260
No. You see, the problem is...

698
00:41:20,678 --> 00:41:22,179
I'm still in love with you.

699
00:41:23,889 --> 00:41:25,224
- You can't...
- Hold on.

700
00:41:25,724 --> 00:41:29,103
I'm not trying to win you back.
You're marrying a good man.

701
00:41:29,895 --> 00:41:31,021
That's not gonna change.

702
00:41:33,065 --> 00:41:34,108
But neither am I.

703
00:41:34,942 --> 00:41:36,860
This whole thing where we pretend
that we're just friends...

704
00:41:36,986 --> 00:41:38,904
it...it doesn't work.

705
00:41:40,531 --> 00:41:43,117
I don't know if it will work
but I know one thing.

706
00:41:44,451 --> 00:41:45,911
I can't watch you marry another man.

707
00:41:48,872 --> 00:41:49,915
All right.

708
00:41:51,750 --> 00:41:52,793
You have a good day...

709
00:41:54,336 --> 00:41:55,379
And a good life.

